          "model_answer": "**Core Conceptual Distinctions:**\n\n1.  **Shareholder Limits**: A private limited company requires a minimum of 2 shareholders and caps the maximum at 200. This structure is designed for closely-held businesses. In contrast, a public limited company requires a minimum of 7 shareholders and has no upper limit on shareholders, allowing it to tap the broader public capital market.\n\n2.  **Paid-Up Capital Requirements**: Regulatory norms stipulate that a private limited company can be started with virtually no minimum paid-up capital (zero requirement). Conversely, a public limited company must have a minimum of five lakh rupees in paid-up capital to commence operations. This threshold acts as a barrier to entry, ensuring a minimum capital base for entities accessing public markets.\n\n3.  **Share Transferability (The Key Distinction)**: The primary differentiating factor between the two entities is the transferability of shares. In a private limited company, share transfer is **restricted**, meaning shareholders cannot easily sell their shares to the general public or outsiders, maintaining ownership control within a small group. In a public limited company, shares are **freely transferable**, allowing investors to buy and sell shares easily, often on stock exchanges, providing liquidity.\n\n4.  **Name Suffix & Liability**: The suffix \"PVT LTD\" denotes a private limited company, while \"LTD\" (without \"Public\") denotes a public limited company. Crucially, the word \"Limited\" in both cases signifies that the liability of the owners is limited (limited by shares), protecting their personal assets from the company's debts and obligations.\n\n**Why share transferability is the primary factor**: While minimum capital and shareholder numbers are quantitative thresholds, share transferability defines the **nature of ownership and control**. It determines whether a company is a closed entity for a few founders (private) or an open entity for public investment (public). This distinction fundamentally alters the governance, compliance burden, and investor rights associated with the company.",
