          "id": 1,
          "question": "Explain the fundamental difference between Shareholder Wealth Maximisation and Profit Maximisation using the concepts of stock and flow variables. Why might a firm maximize profit in the short term while simultaneously destroying shareholder wealth? Provide a brief example.",
          "marks": 10,
          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "The fundamental difference lies in the type of variable being maximized: Profit Maximisation targets a **flow variable** (measured over a period of time), while Shareholder Wealth Maximisation targets a **stock variable** (measured at a point in time). \n\nA firm might maximize short-term profit by cutting R&D or employee training expenses. In the short run, the profit & loss statement shows higher profit (flow variable increases). However, in the long run, the firm's capacity to generate future cash flows diminishes because the underinvestment in R&D and human capital reduces future profitability. Since shareholder wealth is the present value of all future cash flows, destroying the firm's long-term earning potential reduces the stock variable (wealth), even though the flow variable (current profit) spiked.\n\n**Example**: A company stops spending on R&D to save $1 million this year. Current profit increases by $1 million. However, without new products developed, sales decline in years 2 and 3, leading to a total profit loss across those years that exceeds $1 million. The firm's overall value (wealth) has decreased despite the current profit increase.\n\n**Rubric**:\n- **Core conceptual understanding of stock vs. flow (3 marks)**: Correctly identifying the variable types and the core distinction.\n- **Analytical application of the trade-off (4 marks)**: Explaining the short-term gain vs. long-term loss dynamic and the wealth destruction mechanism.\n- **Example relevance and clarity (3 marks)**: Providing a logical example that aligns with the lecture's R&D/training cutting scenario.",
