      "transcript_text": "Now let us move on to our final topic under this broad module of overview of corporate finance. So this topic is on agency costs. Agency costs are rising an agency relationship. What is an agency relationship? An agency relationship is a relationship in which somebody hires somebody else to represent his or her interests. There are two parties. The person who hires and the person who is hired. The person who hires is called as the principal and the person who is hired. is called as an agent of the principal. Now can you think of the relationship between shareholders and managers as a kind of an agency relationship? Yes. In this relationship between shareholders and managers, shareholders and managers, they are agents. Now there is a possibility of conflict of interest between the principal and the agent. And this possibility of conflict of interest is what we call as principal agent problem or agency problem. Why does this problem arise? Because everything which is in the interest of the principal may not be in the interest of the agent. Everything which is in the interest of the shareholders may not necessarily be in the interest of the managers. For example, let us say that there is a company and this company has a value-adding project. However, this project is a risky project. What should the financial manager do ideally? Managers should ideally accept the financial project. Why? Because this project is a value-adding project. If this project is accepted, the wealth of the shareholders will go up. However, what might managers do in the real life? Managers may not accept this value-adding project. Why? Because while this project is value-adding, this project is risky as well. which means that there is some chance that this project might fail. And if this project fails, managers may be fired. And obviously as you can figure out, managers are also human beings. They worry more about their jobs in general than worrying about the interest of shareholders. So this was an example of the agency problem. And therefore, the costs of these conflicts of interest between shareholders and managers are what we call as agency costs. Let me give you some examples of agency costs. Suppose managers incur corporate expenditure which benefits them, but it is unnecessary and obviously it costs shareholders. For example, if managers purchase a luxurious but unneeded corporate jet, it is an example of an agency cost. Let me give you another real life example. Have you heard about a company called as Taiko International? Of course, this company is not existing any more. In early 2000s, the CEO of this company threw a birthday bash for his wife. There is nothing wrong with that. Anybody can throw a birthday bash for one spouse. There is nothing wrong. Now this person spent $2 million on his wife's birthday bash. Again, there is nothing wrong. You can spend as much money as you want on your wife's birthday bash. However, believe it or not, this person charged half of the cost to the company, which means this person charged $1 million to the company. If this is not agency cost, what else it is. Of course, in this case, the agency cost took a very extreme form. It was like a fraud. Also, another example of agency cost will be expense that you need to incur to monitor managerial actions. For example, when you are paying auditors to assess the accuracy of financial statement, this is also kind of an agency cost. Whatever expenses you are incurring to monitor managers. Also, if we think about the cost of lost opportunity, that is also a manifestation of agency cost. If managers have a value adding but risky project, they may shy away from accepting this project and therefore, shareholders are losing a valuable opportunity. And therefore, the cost of lost opportunity is also an agency cost.",
