          "id": 1,
          "question": "With reference to the lecture, explain the three primary mechanisms used to mitigate agency problems, and discuss how a hostile takeover serves as a market-based disciplinary mechanism. (10 marks)",
          "marks": 10,
          "suggested_time_minutes": 15,
          "model_answer": "**Model Answer & Marking Rubric**\n\n**Part 1: Incentive Alignment (4 marks)**\n* Explain that managers can be given employee stock options. \n* Key point: The value of the option increases as the stock price rises, aligning manager interests with shareholders.\n* Examiner's note: Highlight that this motivates value maximization.\n\n**Part 2: Monitoring Mechanisms (3 marks)**\n* Identify the role of the board of directors and auditors in monitoring manager performance.\n* Key point: Mention that these mechanisms are often mandatory by law, providing a baseline of governance structure.\n\n**Part 3: Market Discipline / Hostile Takeovers (3 marks)**\n* Explain that if managers do not pursue shareholder goals, the firm becomes an attractive target for hostile takeovers.\n* Key point: The market itself plays a disciplining role, forcing underperforming managers to either improve or be replaced.\n\n**Rubric Breakdown**\n* **Core conceptual understanding (5 marks):** Accurate identification and description of the three mechanisms (Alignment, Monitoring, Market Discipline). Correct definition of hostile takeover.\n* **Analytical application (5 marks):** Ability to synthesize how these mechanisms interact (e.g., alignment + monitoring + threat of takeover = robust governance). Correct distinction between internal monitoring and external market discipline.",
